result.response:
```novap
tserve service_monitor {
  _services = [
    { name: "auth", port: 8001 },
    { name: "api", port: 8002 },
    // ... 109 more services
    { name: "logs", port: 9101 }
  ]
  _failures = {}

  troute GET /health { http_get("http://localhost:8001/health") }

  tstart {
    while true {
      delay 30s
      for s in _services {
        port_str = str(s.port)
        url = "http://localhost:" + port_str + "/health"
        result = try3 {
          res = http_get(url)
          if str_starts(res.body, "{") { "ok" }
          else { fail "invalid response" }
        } fail {
          _failures = dict_set(_failures, port_str, 
            (dict_get(_failures, port_str) ?: 0) + 1)
          count = dict_get(_failures, port_str)
          if count >= 3 {
            cmd = "systemctl restart srv-" + s.name
            shell_out = shell(cmd)
            log("Restarted " + s.name + ", err: " + shell_out.stderr)
            _failures = dict_set(_failures, port_str, 0)
          }
        }
        if result == "ok" {
          _failures = dict_set(_failures, port_str, 0)
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The service monitor checks 111 services every 30 seconds using `http_get` on their `/health` endpoint. Each service is tracked by port string. On successful response (valid JSON body), failure count resets. If a request fails (via `try3`), the failure counter for that port increments. When a service fails 3 times consecutively, it triggers a shell command to restart the corresponding system service (e.g., `systemctl restart srv-auth`). After restart, the failure count resets to 0. Error handling ensures monitor resilience. Uses `tserve`, `troute`, and `tstart` for service structure. All state stored immutably using `dict_set`. Restarts are logged with stderr output.
